1. COMPLIANCE ENGINE PRINCIPLE
The OneDraft Compliance & Regulatory Engine is responsible for determining whether a specific version of a project model satisfies the applicable regulatory requirements associated with the project jurisdiction.
The Compliance Engine does not create the architectural model.
It does not directly modify geometry.
It does not directly modify project elements.
It does not replace professional review or authority approval.
Its function is to establish a deterministic, traceable relationship between:
Jurisdiction
Regulatory Sources
Code Rules
Project Characteristics
Applicable Requirements
Project Model
Validation Results
Provenance
The fundamental computational chain is:
PROJECT
    ↓
JURISDICTION
    ↓
CODE SOURCES
    ↓
CODE MANIFEST
    ↓
APPLICABILITY RESOLUTION
    ↓
CODE RULE SET
    ↓
PROJECT CHARACTERISTICS
    ↓
MODEL ANALYSIS
    ↓
VALIDATION RESULTS
    ↓
DOCUMENTATION / REVIEW

2. COMPLIANCE ENGINE BOUNDARY
The Compliance Engine is a domain service positioned between the Project Model and the Validation Engine.
The architecture is:
                    ONEDRAFT
                       │
                PROJECT MODEL
                       │
                       ▼
             COMPLIANCE ENGINE
                       │
       ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐
       │               │                │
       ▼               ▼                ▼
 JURISDICTION      CODE MANIFEST    APPLICABILITY
       │               │                │
       └───────────────┼────────────────┘
                       ▼
                  RULE ENGINE
                       │
                       ▼
                 MODEL ANALYSIS
                       │
                       ▼
              VALIDATION RESULTS
                       │
             ┌─────────┴─────────┐
             ▼                   ▼
        DOCUMENTATION        HUMAN REVIEW
The Compliance Engine therefore becomes the regulatory interpretation and analysis layer of OneDraft.

3. CORE DESIGN RULE
The Compliance Engine shall never treat a code source as merely a text document.
A regulatory source must be transformed into structured computational objects.
The system therefore separates:
SOURCE DOCUMENT
        ↓
SOURCE VERSION
        ↓
CODE RULE
        ↓
APPLICABILITY LOGIC
        ↓
VERIFICATION METHOD
        ↓
MODEL TEST
        ↓
RESULT
This allows regulatory requirements to become machine-evaluable without losing their original provenance.

4. REGULATORY PROVENANCE PRINCIPLE
Every compliance determination must be traceable to its regulatory source.
A validation result must be capable of answering:
Which jurisdiction?

Which authority?

Which code source?

Which source version?

Which effective date?

Which rule?

Which applicability condition?

Which project revision?

Which model version?

Which objects were evaluated?

Which calculation was performed?

Which result was produced?

When was the determination produced?
No final compliance result should exist without a provenance chain.

5. IMMUTABLE CODE MANIFEST
The Code Manifest is the regulatory snapshot applicable to a project revision.
Conceptually:
PROJECT
    ↓
CODE MANIFEST
    ├── SOURCE 1
    ├── SOURCE 2
    ├── SOURCE 3
    ├── SOURCE 4
    └── SOURCE N
A Code Manifest contains:
jurisdiction
authority
source identifiers
source versions
effective dates
retrieval timestamps
content hashes
applicable regulatory domains
Once a Code Manifest is attached to a finalized revision, it becomes immutable.
A later regulatory update creates a new manifest.
The previous manifest remains available for historical reconstruction.

6. REGULATORY TIME MODEL
Regulatory applicability is time-dependent.
The engine must distinguish:
publication date
effective date
retrieval date
project date
application date
permit date
revision date
A newer code source must not automatically overwrite the regulatory basis of an earlier project revision.
The compliance engine evaluates the regulatory environment associated with the applicable project timeline.

7. JURISDICTION MODEL
Jurisdiction resolution shall be hierarchical.
Conceptually:
COUNTRY
    ↓
PROVINCE / STATE
    ↓
MUNICIPALITY
    ↓
DISTRICT / AUTHORITY
    ↓
PROJECT
Additional regulatory authorities may apply independently.
Examples include:
building authority
fire authority
accessibility authority
planning authority
energy authority
environmental authority
utility authority
transportation authority
The engine must therefore support multiple simultaneous regulatory authorities.

8. JURISDICTION OBJECT
Conceptually:
Jurisdiction
