30. RULE EXECUTION PIPELINE
The execution pipeline becomes:
LOAD PROJECT
      ↓
LOAD MODEL VERSION
      ↓
LOAD CODE MANIFEST
      ↓
LOAD CODE RULES
      ↓
RESOLVE APPLICABILITY
      ↓
DERIVE PROJECT CHARACTERISTICS
      ↓
EXECUTE RULES
      ↓
CREATE RESULTS
      ↓
CREATE PROVENANCE
      ↓
CREATE SUMMARY
      ↓
COMPLETE RUN

31. RULE EXECUTION IS READ-ONLY
The Compliance Engine shall evaluate the Project Model.
It shall not directly mutate:
elements
spaces
levels
assemblies
geometry
constraints
relationships
drawings
Compliance findings may produce recommendations or commands for Aria, but those commands must pass through the established Project Model command boundary.

32. ARIA COMPLIANCE INTERFACE
Aria may consume compliance results.
Conceptually:
Compliance Result
       ↓
Aria
       ↓
Design Proposal
       ↓
Command
       ↓
Project Model
       ↓
New Revision
       ↓
Compliance Re-run
This prevents the AI from bypassing the transactional model architecture.

33. COMPLIANCE RECOMMENDATIONS
A failed or review-required rule may produce a recommendation.
Conceptually:
Finding
 ↓
Recommendation
 ↓
Design Proposal
A recommendation is not itself a model mutation.

34. COMPLIANCE COMMAND BOUNDARY
The following pattern is mandatory:
COMPLIANCE
    ↓
FINDING
    ↓
RECOMMENDATION
    ↓
ARIA COMMAND
    ↓
COMMAND VALIDATION
    ↓
MODEL TRANSACTION
The Compliance Engine never executes a direct database mutation against the Project Model.

35. CODE MANIFEST CREATION
The manifest generation workflow is:
PROJECT ADDRESS
      ↓
JURISDICTION RESOLUTION
      ↓
AUTHORITY RESOLUTION
      ↓
SOURCE DISCOVERY
      ↓
SOURCE VERSION SELECTION
      ↓
EFFECTIVE-DATE CHECK
      ↓
SOURCE HASH
      ↓
CODE MANIFEST
The resulting manifest receives a UUID.

36. CODE MANIFEST IMMUTABILITY
Once used by a finalized revision:
code_manifest.status = FINAL
The manifest cannot be modified.
A regulatory change creates:
CODE MANIFEST N+1
rather than modifying:
CODE MANIFEST N

37. CODE MANIFEST STATUS
Initial statuses:
DRAFT
ACTIVE
FINAL
SUPERSEDED
ARCHIVED
INVALID
A FINAL manifest is immutable.

38. REGULATORY SOURCE STATUS
Initial source statuses:
DISCOVERED
RETRIEVED
VERIFIED
ACTIVE
SUPERSEDED
ARCHIVED
INVALID
The system must not silently treat an unverified source as authoritative.

39. SOURCE AUTHORITY
Each source records its authority context.
Conceptually:
authority
authority_type
jurisdiction
publication
version
effective_date
The engine uses authority metadata when constructing a Code Manifest.

40. REGULATORY CONFLICTS
Multiple regulatory sources may produce competing requirements.
The engine therefore requires a conflict-resolution layer.
Conceptually:
RULE A
+
RULE B
+
RULE C
     ↓
CONFLICT DETECTION
     ↓
AUTHORITY / APPLICABILITY ANALYSIS
     ↓
RESOLUTION
An unresolved conflict becomes:
REVIEW_REQUIRED
rather than being silently resolved by AI inference.

41. RULE PRIORITY
Rule priority may be determined from:
authority
jurisdiction
effective date
adoption status
specificity
amendment relationship
exception relationship
The priority model must remain explicit.
The engine must never rely on undocumented ordering.

42. EXCEPTIONS
Rules may contain exceptions.
Conceptually:
RULE
 ├── GENERAL REQUIREMENT
 └── EXCEPTION
       └── APPLICABILITY CONDITION
An exception must be evaluated independently.
The engine must record:
exception_id
exception_condition
evaluation_result
provenance

43. THRESHOLD ENGINE
The threshold engine supports:
<
<=
=
>=
>
!=
BETWEEN
OUTSIDE
IN
NOT_IN
Example:
actual = 1.4 m
required >= 1.2 m
Result:
PASS

44. UNIT NORMALIZATION
The Compliance Engine uses canonical internal units.
The OneDraft computational model uses:
millimetres
for internal linear dimensions.
Regulatory rules may use:
mm
cm
m
in
ft
ft-in
The compliance engine normalizes values before evaluation.
Example:
1.2 m
↓
1200 mm
The original regulatory unit remains available for provenance and reporting.

45. DIMENSIONAL VALIDATION
The engine must verify that a comparison is dimensionally valid.
For example:
distance vs distance
area vs area
volume vs volume
height vs height
count vs count
ratio vs ratio
Invalid dimensional comparisons become:
ERROR
rather than producing a numerical result.

46. SPATIAL RULE ENGINE
Certain regulations cannot be evaluated as simple scalar values.
The engine therefore supports spatial predicates.
Initial predicates:
WITHIN
CONTAINS
INTERSECTS
TOUCHES
DISTANCE_FROM
OVERLAPS
SEPARATED_FROM
ALIGNED_WITH
ACCESSIBLE_FROM
CONNECTED_TO
Spatial evaluations operate against the Geometry Engine.

47. SPATIAL ANALYSIS PIPELINE
CODE RULE
     ↓
SPATIAL REQUIREMENT
     ↓
MODEL OBJECT SELECTION
     ↓
GEOMETRY QUERY
     ↓
GEOMETRY RESULT
     ↓
RULE EVALUATION
     ↓
COMPLIANCE RESULT
The Compliance Engine consumes geometry results rather than directly owning geometric computation.

